  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWind subsides during the late afternoon/evening hours as the \nsurface low pivots and begins lifting northeast, though gusts \nwill continue in the ridges. Another round of gusty wind is \nexpected on Friday with cold advection steepening lapse rates in\nlow-level south-southwest (yes) flow. While momentum transfer \nwon't be near as potent as Thursday, we'll still have 25-30 \nknots to mix down and a pressure gradient on the back side of \nthe departing low. Probability for >30 mph gusts ranges from \n50-90%, highest in WV and PA, but >40 mph drops off rapidly to \n20-40% in the lowlands. Eastern Tucker County hangs on to >80% \nprobability for Advisory criteria gusts potentially through \nFriday night, so an extension of wind headlines there will be \nevaluated in the next 12-18 hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nMuch of the region has made its way into the dry slot of the\nevolving system. Some of this dry slot has begun to fill in with\nlighter instances of rain but these are more confined across the\nlowlands than the immediate westerly slopes of the ridges where\ndownsloping rain shadow effects are keeping the area dry for now.\nAs such, there will likely be a minimum in rain amounts along \nthe western slope of the higher elevation in PA down to WV. \nConversely, a pivoting, stronger area of 850-700 mb \nfrontogenesis, and an overall longer period of rain as a \nbackside surface trough slowly pivots through, is likely to \ndrive an area of higher amounts in eastern OH. Think that this \ncorridor is likely to stretch from Youngstown down to Coshocton \nto Zanesville given progged area of frontogenesis maximum and \nconvergence along the nose of the departing LLJ. As expected\nrainfall totals observed thus far rise from west to east, around\n2 inches has already fallen across eastern Ohio, with rain\ncontinuing. Rainfall totals across the northern panhandle of WV\nand the lowlands of western PA are largely around 1.00-1.50 \ninches with the heaviest rain over. Further east along the \ndownslope side of the ridges, rainfall totals are as low as 0.50\ninches. \n\nAs the surface low lifts northeast out of the region through the\nday Friday, expect that the lower elevation remains warm enough\nfor a cool rain, but the ridges could see a mix of rain/snow or\nperiods of all snow showers, especially Eastern Tucker and \nPreston County. Steepening lapse rates plus some lake influences\nalso favors precipitation being more shower-y in nature and \nresult in the \"on and off\" precipitation occurrence intervals.\nMeasurable snow probability showcases around 40-60% in all of \nour ridges and light accumulation of an inch to two is possible\nin Eastern Tucker.\n\nThere is additional upper troughing set to traverse the region\nSunday providing another round of widespread light rain before\nthe region exits the active pattern for a bit, allowing\ntemperature to rise above normal next week.",
